{"study_accession":"PRJNA1439721","secondary_study_accession":"SRP685034","title":"RNA-seq analysis of human peripheral blood Treg cells stratified into four equal fractions according to CD38 intensity","description":"Sensitivity of regulatory T cells (Tregs) to extracellular nucleotides such as ATP and NAD⁺ differs substantially between mice and humans. Mouse Tregs undergo robust apoptosis in response to even low concentrations of ATP or NAD⁺, whereas human Tregs show markedly reduced sensitivity to these nucleotides. In our preliminary analyses, however, we observed that a subset of human Tregs expressing low levels of CD38 exhibited increased sensitivity to ATP/NAD⁺. To investigate the molecular mechanisms underlying this enhanced sensitivity, human peripheral blood Tregs were stratified into four fractions based on CD38 expression levels and subjected to RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) analysis. Overall design: Freshly isolated human peripheral blood regulatory T cells (Tregs) were sorted into four fractions according to CD38 expression levels. RNA was extracted from each fraction and subjected to bulk RNA-seq to identify transcriptional programs associated with increased sensitivity to extracellular nucleotides (ATP/NAD⁺). CD4⁺CD25-high CD27-low Tregs were isolated by fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS) from human peripheral blood.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":12,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN56589101","study_accession":"PRJNA1439721","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"CD38-high Treg RNA-seq replicate 3","description":"CD38-high Treg RNA-seq replicate 3","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM9600938","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2026-05-16","attributes":[["source_name","peripheral blood"],["tissue","peripheral blood"],["cell_type","Regulatory T cell"],["treatment","no treatment"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR37688828","experiment_accession":"SRX32567